ooks super clean. Love the direction you're taking with AI workflows.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Google Antigravity 2.0\",\n    \"url\": \"https://antigravity.google\",\n    \"description\": \"Standalone desktop app for orchestrating multiple AI agents in parallel with scheduled background tasks, subagent workflows, and native integrations with AI Studio, Firebase, and Android. Breaks the 'prompt, wait, respond' pattern by letting agents run in the background on cron-like schedules.\",\n    \"release_date\": \"2026-05-22\",\n    \"category\": \"workflow agent\",\n    \"sources\": [\"Product Hunt\"],\n    \"community_quotes\": [\n      \"Google just separated the agent manager from the IDE and shipped it as its own desktop app.\",\n      \"The cron-like scheduling piece is what I want to understand better. If I schedule a refactor agent to run at 2am, what happens when it hits a decision that needs human judgment?\",\n      \"Parallel agents are powerful, but the monitoring layer is what makes this actually usable.\",\n      \"The subagent workflow is where it gets interesting: running parallel agents without babysitting each one.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenHuman\",\n    \"url\": \"https://tinyhumans.ai/openhuman\",\n    \"description\": \"Open-source AI harness that is local-first, privacy-first, with persistent memory across sessions. Uses a Memory Tree with canonicalization, temporal metadata, and source authority scoring. Integrates with Gmail, Slack, Telegram, Notion, and GitHub. Features 118+ integrations, tool calling with chaining, and a code sandbox.\",\n    \"release_date\": \"2026-05-15\",\n    \"category\": \"open-source agent\",\n    \"sources\": [\"Product Hunt\"],\n    \"community_quotes\": [\n      \"The privacy angle is a big reason to go local-first, but the persistent memory is what actually makes it usable day to day.\",\n      \"I am tired of re-explaining my tech stack and project goals every single time I open a new sess